  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Washington received $13B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 80,705 awards with an average award size of $161K, and obligations fell 17.8% year over year. Obligations by fiscal year show a multi-year pattern: FY21 totaled $15.6B, FY22 totaled $12.1B, FY23 totaled $13B, FY24 totaled $15.4B, FY25 totaled $15.8B, and FY26 totaled $13B to date. Aircraft Manufacturing leads industry spending at $5.9B, followed by Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $1.2B. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $8.7B in obligations, and THE BOEING COMPANY is the largest vendor with $5.9B. Energy and environmental work also shows up strongly, with the Department of Energy at $3.1B and several cleanup and remediation contractors and industries appearing in the top ranks. These figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial because FY2026 is fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How have federal contract obligations in Washington trended recently?",
        "body": "Obligations in Washington were $15.8B in FY25 and fell to $13B in FY26 to date. Obligations were $15.4B in FY24, $13B in FY23, $12.1B in FY22, and $15.6B in FY21. Overall, obligations fell 17.8% year over year for the current FY2026 to date period."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries dominate Washington contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $8.7B in FY2026 to date and the Department of Energy obligated $3.1B. Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest industry at $5.9B, and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences is second at $1.2B. Other sizable industry categories include Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $746M and Hazardous Waste Treatment and Disposal at $698M."
      }
    ]
  },
